    "meal_prep": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "m_sub": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "Make it ahead",
            "subheading_size": "subtitle-md",
            "secondary_color": true
          }
        },
        "m_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why the fish gets slashed before it goes in",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Two or three cuts on each side, down to the bone. It is not decorative. A whole fish is thick behind the head and thin at the tail, and the slashes let heat into the deepest part so the whole thing finishes at roughly the same moment instead of overcooking at one end.</p><p>They also give you somewhere to look. Rather than hacking into the fish to see whether it is done, you open one slash and check whether the flesh flakes &mdash; alongside 135&deg;F at the thickest point, that is the whole doneness test.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_btn": {
          "type": "button",
          "settings": {
            "button_label": "Shop Whole Trout",
            "button_link": "/products/whole-trout-12oz",
            "button_style": "primary",
            "button_size": "md",
            "button_external": false,
            "button_icon": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "m_sub",
        "m_head",
        "m_text",
        "m_btn"
      ],
