    "faq_trout": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_scared": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "I have never cooked a whole fish. Is this really a good place to start?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, and for a specific reason: a sheet pan gives you no chance to fuss. There is no flipping, no pan to stick to and no window where it can go wrong quickly. The roasting is easier than a fillet. The only genuinely new skill is getting it off the bone afterwards, and that is three movements &mdash; see the section above.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when a whole trout is cooked?",
            "text": "<p>135&deg;F at the thickest part, just behind the head, which is the last place to come up to temperature. Without a thermometer, look into one of the slashes you cut before roasting &mdash; the flesh should be opaque and flake away from the bone rather than clinging to it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_timing": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I just put everything in at once?",
            "text": "<p>You can, and you will get one of three problems. The grapes will have spent nearly half an hour in the oven and collapsed into jam, or the fish will be raw at the spine, or the potatoes will be undercooked. The stagger exists precisely because the three want different amounts of heat.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_grapes": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Grapes with fish? Really.",
            "text": "<p>Really. Roasted, they stop being sweet in a fruit way and turn jammy and slightly savoury &mdash; closer to a warm dressing than to dessert. They burst against the potatoes and the almond butter, and the acidity does the same job a squeeze of lemon would. It is the least obvious thing on the pan and the thing people ask about.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_head": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I have to leave the head on?",
            "text": "<p>No, but it helps. The head holds the cavity open so the aromatics stay put, it gives you something to grip when you lift the skeleton out, and it keeps moisture in the collar, which is the best bite on the fish. If it bothers you at the table, take it off after roasting rather than before.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat it very dry inside and out. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_scared",
        "q_done",
        "q_timing",
        "q_grapes",
        "q_head",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
